{
  "id": "m09-two-lonely",
  "title": "Two Lonely Numbers",
  "source": {
    "name": "Classic XOR trick (single number III)"
  },
  "rating": 1200,
  "tags": [
    "bitmask",
    "xor",
    "bit-manipulation"
  ],
  "statement": "An array of `n` integers contains exactly two values that appear **once**; every other value appears **exactly twice**. Find the two lonely values.\n\n**Input.** First line `n`. Second line `n` integers.\n\n**Output.** The two lonely values in increasing order, separated by a space.",
  "constraints": [
    "2 ≤ n ≤ 4000",
    "0 ≤ a_i ≤ 10^9",
    "The array has the described shape."
  ],
  "timeLimitMs": 2000,
  "hints": [
    "A sorted copy or a hash map works; but is there a way with `O(1)` extra memory using xor?",
    "The xor of the whole array equals `p ^ q` for the two lonely values. Since `p != q`, some bit is set in this xor.",
    "Take `low = x & -x`. That bit differs between `p` and `q`, so splitting the array by that bit separates them; xor each group."
  ],
  "editorial": "Pairs cancel under xor, so `x = p ^ q` and `x != 0`. Let `low = x & -x`, a bit where `p` and `q` differ. Every duplicated value lands in one group as a pair (both copies have the same bit), so xoring the group with bit `low` set gives exactly one of `p, q`, and the other group gives the other. One pass, `O(n)` time, `O(1)` memory.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(1)"
  },
  "starter": {
    "python": "n = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\n# print the two lonely values in increasing order\n",
    "javascript": "const n = parseInt(readLine());\nconst a = readLine().split(' ').map(Number);\n// print the two lonely values in increasing order\n",
    "java": "import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int n = sc.nextInt();\n        // read n ints\n    }\n}\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int n; cin >> n;  // xor everything, split by the lowest set bit\n    return 0;\n}\n"
  },
  "solutions": {
    "python": "n = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\nx = 0\nfor v in a:\n    x ^= v\nlow = x & -x\np = q = 0\nfor v in a:\n    if v & low:\n        p ^= v\n    else:\n        q ^= v\nprint(min(p, q), max(p, q))\n",
    "javascript": "const n = parseInt(readLine());\nconst a = readLine().split(' ').map(Number);\nlet x = 0;\nfor (const v of a) x ^= v;\nconst low = x & -x;\nlet p = 0, q = 0;\nfor (const v of a) {\n  if (v & low) p ^= v; else q ^= v;\n}\nconsole.log(Math.min(p, q) + ' ' + Math.max(p, q));\n",
    "java": "import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int n = sc.nextInt();\n        int[] a = new int[n];\n        int x = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { a[i] = sc.nextInt(); x ^= a[i]; }\n        int low = x & -x;\n        int p = 0, q = 0;\n        for (int v : a) {\n            if ((v & low) != 0) p ^= v; else q ^= v;\n        }\n        System.out.println(Math.min(p, q) + \" \" + Math.max(p, q));\n    }\n}\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    cin >> n;\n    vector<int> a(n);\n    int x = 0;\n    for (auto &v : a) {\n        cin >> v;\n        x ^= v;\n    }\n    int low = x & -x;\n    int p = 0, q = 0;\n    for (int v : a) {\n        if (v & low) p ^= v;\n        else q ^= v;\n    }\n    cout << min(p, q) << \" \" << max(p, q) << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n"
  },
